  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1200 AM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nAn overall pattern shift is expected for next week as models\ncontinue to see a decent upper-level trough push through the\nPacific Northwest and into the Northern Rocky Mountains. While\nthis trough is not expected to dig too far south during its\npassage, it will force the upper-level high to finally retreat\nfurther east and open a majority of Colorado to southwesterly\nflow and more monsoonal moisture. For Monday, present model\nagreement continues to see a weak cold frontal passage make its\nway south and across Colorado. Present runs of the models are\nstill a little back and forth on the exact timing of the front\nentering Southern Colorado. However, present trends do see this\nfront opening us up for better opportunities for showers and\nthunderstorms to develop across the plains and the I-25 for the\nday. These increase chances of seeing showers and thunderstorms\nlook to hold through the rest of the week as models see another\nupper-level trough build just off the West Coast and keep\nSouthern Colorado open to moisture and short-wave activity.\nCurrently our best chances continue to trend for Tuesday and\nWednesday night into Thursday with areas around the I-25\ncorridor and eastern plains seeing chances around 40 to 60\npercent. Unfortunately these chances will also come with\nincrease risk of flash flooding on present burn scars in our\narea. Still, this extra activity alongside some timely frontal\npassage Monday and Wednesday will see cool temperatures for the\nweek, with present afternoon highs in the valleys and plains\nholding in the upper 70s to mid 80s starting Tuesday.",
